<p class="para lt-chem-349740" id="av_1.0-ch14_s03_s02_p12">Knowing the rate constant for the hydrolysis of cisplatin and the rate constants for subsequent reactions that produce species that are highly toxic enables hospital pharmacists to provide patients with solutions that contain only the desired form of the drug.</p>

<div class="exercises block" id="av_1.0-ch14_s03_s02_n02">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Book:_General_Chemistry:_Principles_Patterns_and_Applications_(Averill)/14:_Chemical_Kinetics/14.04:_Methods_of_Determining_Reaction_Order" class="mt-section"><span id="Example_14.3.1"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-349740 editable">Example 14.3.1</h3>

<p class="para lt-chem-349740" id="av_1.0-ch14_s03_s02_p13">At high temperatures, ethyl chloride produces HCl and ethylene by the following reaction:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-349740">\( CH_{3}CH_{2}Cl\left ( g \right )\overset{\Delta }{\rightarrow} HCl\left ( g \right )+C_{2}H_{4}\left ( g \right ) \)</p>

<p class="para lt-chem-349740" id="av_1.0-ch14_s03_s02_p14">Using the rate data for the reaction at 650&deg;C presented in the following table, calculate the reaction order with respect to the concentration of ethyl chloride and determine the rate constant for the reaction.</p>

